fundamental issue with um say bitcoin in  its current form  uh in that it's the transaction volume  is very limited latency for  properly confirmed transactions to is  too long much longer than you'd like  it's actually not great from um  a transaction volume standpoint or a  latency standpoint  so it is perhaps useful as to search to  solve an aspect of the money database  problem which is the sort of store of  wealth or an  accounting of  relative obligations i suppose but it is  not useful as a  currency as a day-to-day currency one of  the things that i think is really  interesting is that obviously elon is  talking about the layer one of bitcoin  and of course they have about 10 minute  block times and if there's tons and tons  of people trying to use the layer one  it's not super scalable so fees spike  and the wait time can be quite long but  that is true a couple of years ago now  we have things like the lightning  network layer two and this is gonna be  true of all blockchains not just bitcoin  but every single blockchain is gonna  eventually run into the problem you  cannot scale a layer one you're going to  have to use layer 1 for security it's  all about protecting the network being  as decentralized as possible the  scalability is going to come in at layer  2. so if you look at something like the  lightning network you can have  instantaneous cash finality settlements  to those transactions and you can do it  for essentially no cost so the  scalability the low cost the high  throughput all happens at layer two and  it benefits from the security of layer  one i think that elon may not understand  exactly what's going on with the layer  two and he's just focused on the layer  one the other thing that i think is  really interesting is i've talked to a  number of people who were either  founders or early employees at paypal  where elon obviously thought a lot about  money and one of the things that i find  interesting is many of them separate out  the store of value asset from a medium  of exchange asset now in my opinion  actually the holy grail is to be able to  do both if you think of the dollar for  example it serves as a store value  sometimes a good one sometimes not it  serves as a medium of exchange and also  serves as a unit of account those are  basically the three aspects of money  store value medium exchange and a unit  of account now bitcoin today is much  better as a store value than it is as a  medium of exchange but ultimately so was  the dollar at one point the dollar was  backed by gold gold was a great store of  value it sucked as a medium of exchange  dollars were created on top of the gold  as paper claims to that gold the dollars  were the layer two the dollars made it  easier to have a medium of exchange so  ultimately that's what bitcoin is going  through as well we're only 12 years into  this journey and there is a sequence to  the game there's levels to this [ __ ] as  some may say you start with the store of  value at layer one that's all about  security you then build layer two and  that's where you get medium of exchange  with things like the lightning network  and then you continue to go from there  so ultimately i think that bitcoin is  following the exact same sequence that  other forms of money have followed in  the past elon then went on and started  to talk about dogecoin which we know  he's super excited about and we know  that he holds a very small portion of  his portfolio in dogecoin as well so  here's what elon had to say about doge  specifically part reason why i think  there's some  um merits dogecoin even though it was  obviously created as a joke is that  it it actually does have a much higher  uh transaction volume capability than  bitcoin you know like the cost of doing  a transaction the the doj coin fee is is  very low like right now if you want to  do a bitcoin transaction the price of  doing that transaction is very high so  you could not use it effectively for  most things um and nor could it even  scale to a high volume and when bitcoin  was you know started i guess when around  2008 or something like that the internet  connections were  much worse than they are today like  order of magnitude i mean it's just the  way way worse you know in 2008 so  so like having us you know a small lock  size or whatever is  you know and a long  synchronization time is  made sense in 2008 but did you know 2021  or fast forward 10 years it's like it's  it's like economically low and i think  there's some  value to  having  a linear increase in the amount of  currency that  is generated if if a currency is  expected to increase in value over time  there's a reluctance to spend it because  you're like oh if i  i'll just hold it and not spend it  because it's scarcity is increasing with  time so if i spend it now then i will  request defending it stop it there  here's two things that i think i'm  pulling out of his comments around  douchecoin the first is this idea that  dogecoin has higher throughput and lower  fees well the way that a blockchain  works is that if nobody uses it the fee  is essentially zero because the fee is a  direct response to competition for that  block space now at the layer one if you  compare bitcoin to dogecoin nobody uses  dogecoin and lots of people use bitcoin  so naturally the fee is going to be  higher another way to look at it is that  even though dogecoin's fees are nearly  nothing compared to bitcoin nobody is  still using it in comparison from an  aggregate number of users or aggregate  number of transactions the second thing  obviously is that the people who are  using bitcoin very frequently they're  starting to use layer two compared to  dogecoin's layer one it's important to  understand yes it has lower fees but  that is not necessarily a good thing  obviously if it was super popular the  fees would go up and the reason why the  fees are down is because it's not super  popular as a medium of exchange the  second thing is that when we start to  get into ideas around bitcoin you could  increase the block size you could make  all of these changes but the important  part about bitcoin is not only that the  layer one is secure that it's the most  decentralized strongest computer network  in the world but also that the monetary  policy and the technical components of  the actual blockchain are not going to  change what we know is that central  banks need to be both independent and  predictable in order to be effective  there is nothing more independent for a  monetary policy than to be decentralized  no one person or group controls it but  also to be predictable you can't change  you can't have variables coming into  your monetary policy if you think about  in the united states what's the next  interest rate decision nobody knows how  much qe is coming next year nobody knows  but with bitcoin we know exactly what  can happen we know exactly what the  design of the system is and the  inability to change it by any one person  or group is part of the value prop it is  predictable it's a programmatic monetary  policy and that is part of the value  that people choose to understand and use  bitcoin as a store of value now let's  continue with elon's comments some  dilution of the currency occurring over  time that's that's more of an incentive  to use as a currency those coins so it  randomly has  uh  a just a fixed a number of sort of coins  or hash strings that are generated every  year so there's there's some inflation  but it's not a percentage base it's a  it's so  it's a fixed number so the percentage of  inflation will necessarily decline over  time i'm not saying that it's like the  ideal system for a currency but i think  it actually is just fundamentally better  than anything else i've seen just by  accident i don't know how much time elon  is spending on cryptocurrency bitcoin  dogecoin etc he's kind of a busy guy  been super super successful i'm a big  fan he's obviously built one of the most  important car companies in america he  also happens to be launching reusable  rockets and then landing them back on  drone ships in the ocean it's absolutely  incredible but i think that when it  comes to this part of his legacy  knowledge around money his legacy  understanding of transactions and his  understanding of how the fiat system  works may actually be somewhat of a  liability it's limiting the way that he  understands how some of this works now i  don't disagree with him that the ideas  of competition and experimentation are  important in the industry but i do think  that he is underestimating the  importance of bitcoin both the layer one  the scalability of layer two and also  the fact that the network effect has  taken hold and there are 100 plus  million people around the world that are  choosing to use bitcoin as a global  store of value now that medium of  exchange use case that he talked about  will continue to become more and more  popular as adoption increases for  bitcoin because as adoption increases  the volatility ends up going down as  volatility goes down the opportunity  cost of holding versus spending  drastically decreases as well one of the  critiques if i was elon that i would  actually talk about in terms of  preventing more and more medium of uh  exchange use cases is that there's  taxation on it it's not like a  traditional currency where if you go and  you spend it you don't pay capital gains  tax with bitcoin you do same with  dogecoin and others so i do think that  there are fair critiques of these assets  but i think that elon's comments are on  money dead-on he really understands  money he understands what makes it  powerful he understands what drives that  value he also understands generally  blockchains and cryptocurrency he  realizes that this is a database  technology that can help solve some of  the problems with legacy money but i do  think that he's underestimating bitcoin  and i hope that as he continues to learn  more then he'll continue to evolve his  thought process i've got a big big  belief that elon's eventually going to  continue to become an expert on this  topic as well

